00:01:44.188 --> 00:01:46.787
- [Steve] Maurizio asks "I'm a self-taught

00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:48.181
"social media marketer.

00:01:48.181 --> 00:01:49.504
"Is it worth doing a course on it

00:01:49.504 --> 00:01:50.777
"when I'm applying for jobs?"

00:01:51.502 --> 00:01:53.756
- Maurizio, you know it's
an interesting question.

00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:55.793
I'm self-taught.

00:01:55.793 --> 00:01:57.893
I didn't take any courses in social media.

00:01:57.893 --> 00:01:59.583
I think it's done okay for me.

00:02:00.564 --> 00:02:03.143
But in general, I'm not
a good student either.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:06.005
I never took a real substantial course

00:02:06.005 --> 00:02:07.374
in business or marketing either

00:02:07.374 --> 00:02:09.372
and that's worked out all right for me.

00:02:09.372 --> 00:02:11.995
I think this is an answer
that really matters

00:02:11.995 --> 00:02:13.822
based on being self aware.

00:02:14.315 --> 00:02:16.218
I think if you find yourself as somebody

00:02:16.218 --> 00:02:20.944
who, in general, being a
self-taught social media expert,

00:02:20.944 --> 00:02:22.508
in general, I'm cynical to that.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:25.271
I think 99% of you are clowns

00:02:25.271 --> 00:02:27.372
and are just reading headlines

00:02:27.372 --> 00:02:30.228
and are not practitioners
and don't go deep.

00:02:30.228 --> 00:02:31.971
I'm even scared of you taking a course

00:02:31.971 --> 00:02:34.048
because most of the courses I've seen

00:02:34.048 --> 00:02:36.207
when I've come and spoke at that class,

00:02:36.207 --> 00:02:37.426
when I vetted the teacher through

00:02:37.426 --> 00:02:40.363
the, them interviewing me process,

00:02:40.363 --> 00:02:42.371
I've realized they were clowns.

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:44.949
So in a whole Ringling Brothers
and Barnum &amp; Bailey Circus

00:02:44.949 --> 00:02:48.129
kind of environment, it all scares me.

00:02:48.129 --> 00:02:50.068
I would tell you the thing
that most matters to me

00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:52.843
is to become the most surgical deepest

00:02:52.843 --> 00:02:56.209
knowledgeable practitioner you can be.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.707
But I can't really answer this for you.

00:02:57.707 --> 00:02:58.485
There's too many variables.

00:02:58.485 --> 00:03:00.748
One, are the courses good?

00:03:00.748 --> 00:03:04.162
Two, are you the type
that actually can learn

00:03:04.162 --> 00:03:05.183
in that environment?

00:03:05.183 --> 00:03:06.344
I can't.

00:03:06.344 --> 00:03:08.062
On the flip side, by being a practitioner,

00:03:08.062 --> 00:03:09.386
that's the best way I learn.

00:03:09.386 --> 00:03:12.160
So that's a whole lot of ego and bravado

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.553
and I apologize for all
my listeners on iTunes

00:03:14.553 --> 00:03:17.154
that aren't used to this but
I'm just spitting the truth.

00:03:17.154 --> 00:03:20.090
Social media right now is in
a very awkward early stage.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.225
If you go back and look at
the early internet marketers

00:03:23.225 --> 00:03:25.291
of 1995, six, seven, eight, nine,

00:03:25.291 --> 00:03:26.964
they were spewing a lot of crap as well.

00:03:26.964 --> 00:03:28.508
So it's a difficult time.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.247
I would say this, in five years I'd feel

00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:32.867
a hell of a lot more comfortable
you taking that course.

00:03:33.638 --> 00:03:35.704
- [Steve] Chase asks "On an average day,

00:03:35.704 --> 00:03:37.551
"how many impressions do your tweets get?"

00:03:38.845 --> 00:03:39.682
- Chase.

00:03:39.682 --> 00:03:40.528
- [Steve] Oh I was gonna say--

00:03:40.528 --> 00:03:42.768
- Chase, stick there Steve.

00:03:42.768 --> 00:03:44.603
Chase, this man has done the work,

00:03:44.603 --> 00:03:45.301
give him the credit.

00:03:45.301 --> 00:03:45.985
Give him the air time.

00:03:45.985 --> 00:03:46.969
Steve.

00:03:48.273 --> 00:03:49.330
What is, what is the answer?

00:03:49.936 --> 00:03:54.906
- So, his 28-day average is
240,000 impressions per day

00:03:55.381 --> 00:03:58.929
and that's 6.7 million
over the last 28 days.

00:03:59.642 --> 00:04:01.453
- There you go, that is the answer.

00:04:01.453 --> 00:04:03.194
And let's give you a better answer

00:04:03.194 --> 00:04:05.458
to everybody overall because
I'm not sure what that means

00:04:05.458 --> 00:04:08.801
or if we care, the more
important question is

00:04:08.801 --> 00:04:10.694
"How many of those impressions cared?"

00:04:10.694 --> 00:04:14.014
What I can tell you is in 2011
when I had 100,000 followers

00:04:14.014 --> 00:04:16.278
on Twitter, I was getting more engagement,

00:04:16.278 --> 00:04:17.973
more interaction, selling more books,

00:04:17.973 --> 00:04:20.839
getting more people to watch
my videos because of it.

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:21.988
This speaks to the thing

00:04:21.988 --> 00:04:23.499
that I most care about in the world.

00:04:23.499 --> 00:04:26.158
The supply and demand of attention.

00:04:26.158 --> 00:04:27.631
Nothing else matters.

00:04:27.631 --> 00:04:29.535
Going to platforms early on

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.416
when there's early tribes there

00:04:31.416 --> 00:04:33.076
and they're paying more attention,

00:04:33.076 --> 00:04:37.777
that is to me the upside of
jumping into Snapchat early.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:41.353
The upside of jumping into
new platforms like Vine early.

00:04:41.353 --> 00:04:44.372
You look at the first people
that over indexed it on Vine,

00:04:44.372 --> 00:04:46.914
they are massively
internet famous right now

00:04:46.914 --> 00:04:48.713
on YouTube and Snapchat and Instagram

00:04:48.713 --> 00:04:50.779
and other places along with Vine.

00:04:50.779 --> 00:04:52.835
The ones that are popping now on Vine

00:04:52.835 --> 00:04:55.156
are not getting to that same level.

00:04:55.156 --> 00:04:57.756
So, the impressions,
the reach, it matters,

00:04:57.756 --> 00:04:59.568
but the depth is what matters the most

00:04:59.568 --> 00:05:02.412
and more importantly, the
attention of that consumer

00:05:02.412 --> 00:05:03.573
on that platform.

00:05:03.573 --> 00:05:05.988
When something's new,
it's a little more sticky.

00:05:05.988 --> 00:05:07.311
When a new song comes out,

00:05:07.311 --> 00:05:08.994
you listen to it a bunch of times

00:05:08.994 --> 00:05:10.735
and then it gets into rotation.

00:05:10.735 --> 00:05:13.057
Twitter right now is in rotation

00:05:13.057 --> 00:05:14.729
in a social media world

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:16.934
versus where it was
four or five years ago.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:18.954
So though my top line followers are more,

00:05:18.954 --> 00:05:20.475
it's my depth that I worry about

00:05:20.475 --> 00:05:22.368
and that is a thesis and a strategy

00:05:22.368 --> 00:05:25.999
that all of you need to
figure out across the board.

00:05:26.872 --> 00:05:29.193
- [Steve] Robert asks "Back
in the old school days

00:05:29.193 --> 00:05:32.358
"of hip hop, were you East
Coast, West Coast, or both?"

00:05:33.373 --> 00:05:36.670
- Robert, Biggie changed
my life so I'm East Coast.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:37.611
But I will tell you something.

00:05:37.611 --> 00:05:38.946
I'm gonna throw you for a real curve ball.

00:05:38.946 --> 00:05:41.325
Specially cause it's good
times in Cleveland right now.

00:05:41.325 --> 00:05:43.369
I'm a little bit more Cleveland.

00:05:43.369 --> 00:05:47.107
I was all in, I mean like let's
put all the pieces in here.

00:05:47.107 --> 00:05:52.096
I was all in on Bone Thugs-N-Harmony.

00:05:52.096 --> 00:05:53.667
Take me to the cross roads,

00:05:53.667 --> 00:05:57.721
every time East 99 is
where you'll find me.

00:05:59.456 --> 00:06:01.703
So I'm a little bit more Cleveland.

00:06:02.196 --> 00:06:03.937
- Hey Gary, it's Jason Calacanis.

00:06:03.937 --> 00:06:06.515
Love the new show and
just a question for you.

00:06:06.515 --> 00:06:08.035
Short or long videos?

00:06:08.035 --> 00:06:09.797
I like long ones, you do short ones.

00:06:09.797 --> 00:06:11.376
Who's right in this situation?

00:06:11.376 --> 00:06:14.278
What's the value of
short versus long videos?

00:06:14.278 --> 00:06:15.044
Explain.

00:06:15.044 --> 00:06:16.879
- Great question @Jason.

00:06:16.879 --> 00:06:19.201
Fun to see some of the
internet famous peeps

00:06:19.201 --> 00:06:20.671
showing up on the show.

00:06:21.384 --> 00:06:22.557
You know, it's funny, I hear him say

00:06:22.557 --> 00:06:24.379
"I like long ones, you like short ones."

00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:27.281
Wine Library TV was 30 minutes everyday

00:06:27.281 --> 00:06:28.976
so I've been in the long game

00:06:28.976 --> 00:06:31.101
unless he's talking an hour which is fine.

00:06:31.101 --> 00:06:32.607
But like now we're
getting into nitpicking.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:34.833
I like both.

00:06:35.744 --> 00:06:37.126
Here's what I would say.

00:06:37.126 --> 00:06:39.668
Avatar, three-plus hour movie.

00:06:39.668 --> 00:06:41.804
People sat, listened to it, loved it.

00:06:41.804 --> 00:06:43.407
A tremendous Jerome Jarre,

00:06:43.407 --> 00:06:44.591
my partner in GrapeStory,

00:06:44.591 --> 00:06:48.204
six-second Vine video, people
love it, sit through it.

00:06:48.204 --> 00:06:51.189
I actually think, Jason,
and VaynerNation,

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:56.337
that length has no variable on quality.

00:06:56.337 --> 00:06:57.997
You need to play within your length

00:06:57.997 --> 00:07:00.958
but you can watch an hour
and forty-minute movie

00:07:00.958 --> 00:07:02.478
and think it sucks, right?

00:07:02.478 --> 00:07:05.238
Or you can watch a six-second
Vine and think it sucks

00:07:05.238 --> 00:07:09.079
to counter my earlier point
of those quality outputs

00:07:09.079 --> 00:07:10.543
within those time lengths.

00:07:10.543 --> 00:07:14.571
So, to me, which one's better?

00:07:14.571 --> 00:07:17.404
Both, cause I'm a positive guy.

00:07:17.404 --> 00:07:18.864
Somebody would say neither.

00:07:19.495 --> 00:07:22.536
And I think it comes down
to what are you doing

00:07:22.536 --> 00:07:25.311
within those constraints and
I think it becomes contextual.

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:27.855
The skill it takes to make
three-hour feature film

00:07:27.855 --> 00:07:29.627
is very different than the skill it takes

00:07:29.627 --> 00:07:31.532
to make a six-second Vine video

00:07:31.532 --> 00:07:32.929
or 15-second Instagram video

00:07:32.929 --> 00:07:34.595
to capture somebody's attention.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:37.607
So, that's my answer.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:40.323
- [Steve] George asks "What's your take

00:07:40.323 --> 00:07:42.303
"on marketing automation software?"

00:07:44.607 --> 00:07:46.128
- George, the key here is, you know,

00:07:46.128 --> 00:07:48.310
I'm not a huge fan of automation.

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:50.029
I've talked about that ad nauseam.

00:07:50.029 --> 00:07:51.548
Matter of fact, once and for all,

00:07:51.548 --> 00:07:53.127
because it keeps coming up,

00:07:53.127 --> 00:07:55.798
and I know a lot of people are
now new listeners on iTunes.

00:07:55.798 --> 00:07:57.853
If you ever get a Tweet from me

00:07:57.853 --> 00:07:59.420
or reply to a YouTube comment

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:00.878
or a reply in the Facebook comments

00:08:00.878 --> 00:08:03.176
like I did all weekend, it is me.

00:08:03.176 --> 00:08:05.403
There is no outsourcing of my engagement.

00:08:05.403 --> 00:08:07.354
I had to make a video
to prove it this weekend.

00:08:07.354 --> 00:08:08.362
I don't know if you guys saw that.

00:08:08.362 --> 00:08:10.264
Like to prove that it was me Tweeting.

00:08:10.264 --> 00:08:11.136
It is me.

00:08:11.931 --> 00:08:15.029
The way to humanize automation for people

00:08:15.029 --> 00:08:18.658
that want to send out
things is the follow-up.

00:08:19.105 --> 00:08:21.706
So if you put something
out and you schedule it,

00:08:21.706 --> 00:08:23.308
not my style, I'm against that.

00:08:23.308 --> 00:08:25.350
Because here's what's
dangerous about automation,

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.072
specially in social and email,

00:08:28.877 --> 00:08:30.676
for all the people, I had a lot of friends

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:31.757
and I killed them on this.

00:08:31.757 --> 00:08:33.313
Let's go back to a very sad day.

00:08:33.702 --> 00:08:38.673
The Boston Marathon massacre, right.

00:08:39.828 --> 00:08:42.533
After that happened, over
the next hour or two,

00:08:42.533 --> 00:08:44.006
the amount of people that Tweeted

00:08:44.006 --> 00:08:48.109
promotional buy my
book, check out my show,

00:08:48.614 --> 00:08:50.959
sign up for my course, my friends.

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:52.851
I was emailing and DMing them saying

00:08:52.851 --> 00:08:54.719
"You are ruining your brand.

00:08:54.719 --> 00:08:57.297
"There are forty people
right now that will never

00:08:57.297 --> 00:08:59.701
"respect you again because
of what you're doing."

00:08:59.701 --> 00:09:01.934
So in a world where
everything is real time,

00:09:02.555 --> 00:09:04.981
scheduling is dangerous
for those anomaly moments

00:09:04.981 --> 00:09:06.258
where you can look really bad.

00:09:06.258 --> 00:09:08.754
Don't let mainstream media pick you up

00:09:08.754 --> 00:09:10.112
that you're promoting your book

00:09:10.112 --> 00:09:11.772
after the President's been shot

00:09:11.772 --> 00:09:13.340
or a building's been exploded

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:15.499
or terrorist act happened
because that can be the end

00:09:15.499 --> 00:09:16.402
of your career completely.

00:09:16.402 --> 00:09:19.002
To me that is not worth the
upside of the automation.

00:09:19.002 --> 00:09:21.103
But if you go the automation route, fine.

00:09:21.103 --> 00:09:22.368
Here's how you make it human.

00:09:22.368 --> 00:09:23.668
You act human behind it.

00:09:23.668 --> 00:09:25.724
You put something out,
people are engaging with it,

00:09:25.724 --> 00:09:28.301
you come in humanly and engage it.

00:09:28.301 --> 00:09:30.739
This will be always a debate
that I'm in a minority

00:09:30.739 --> 00:09:33.502
which is I want to scale the unscalable

00:09:33.502 --> 00:09:35.104
in a world where people
are trying to use modern

00:09:35.104 --> 00:09:37.298
technology to scale.

00:09:37.298 --> 00:09:38.389
It's as simple as that.

00:09:38.389 --> 00:09:39.399
I'm counter cultural.

00:09:39.399 --> 00:09:42.220
I'm over here, you're over here.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.482
You're over here, I'm over here

00:09:45.482 --> 00:09:47.828
and I will stay here
because I believe in it

00:09:47.828 --> 00:09:50.648
and I believe in it not
because I'm romantic

00:09:50.648 --> 00:09:54.107
or Zen or such a great guy.

00:09:54.107 --> 00:09:57.044
I believe in it because
I think it sells shit.

00:09:57.654 --> 00:09:59.533
You've been watching Episode 31

00:09:59.533 --> 00:10:00.671
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

